 the data for Jan. 1981 (Treasury bills - regular weekly and annual):\n\n\nI'll analyze the holdings for each investor category carefully. The challenge is interpreting the data correctly, considering the asterisk (*) for insurance companies and understanding the precise breakdown of state and local government holdings. I need to verify whether the categories match the problem's specified seven investor types and determine their Treasury bill holdings for January 1981.\n\nThe table reveals a complex classification system with multiple subcategories under broader investor groups. I'll need to map these subcategories against the seven specified categories to accurately assess their Treasury bill holdings and determine which ones exceed the $200 million threshold.\n\nThe data shows specific holdings for January 1981, with most categories having minimal investments. The commercial banks stand out as the primary investor, while most other categories show negligible or zero holdings. I'll carefully evaluate each category's investment status relative to the $200 million benchmark.\n\nThe insurance companies and savings and loan associations are marked with an asterisk, indicating minimal to no investment. Only commercial banks significantly exceed the threshold, suggesting limited market participation during this period.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_36d973dbc150fca1","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"cat /app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1980_03.json | python3 -c \"\nimport json\nimport sys\ndata = json.load(sys.stdin)\n\n# Get the table with Jan 1981 data for Treasury bills - regular weekly and annual maturing\ntable = data['document']['elements'][910]['content']\n\n# Let's parse all the Treasury bills rows to find the Jan. 1981 one\nimport re\n# Find all table rows\nrows = re.findall(r'<tr><td>([^<]+)</td><td>([^<]+)</td>(.*?)</tr>', table)\n\nprint('Looking for Jan 1981 Treasury bills row:')\nfor row in 